 "equipment_type": "Boiler / water heater controller",
 "applies_to": "AERCO C-More boiler controllers (software 2.00 or later) used on Benchmark and KC Series boilers and water heaters, as a Modbus RTU slave.",
 "summary": "The C-More controller on AERCO Benchmark and KC Series units is a Modbus RTU slave on RS-485, normally driven by an AERCO BMS/ACS or a third-party EMS. A master can monitor temperatures, firing rate, flame strength, run hours and status messages, and can control the unit either by writing a network setpoint (Remote Setpoint mode) or a firing rate (Direct Drive mode). Temperatures are transferred as 0-1000 counts that must be converted with AERCO's equations.",
 "transport": [
  "RTU"
 ],
 "defaults": {
  "unit_id": null,
  "baud": 9600,
  "parity": null,
  "data_bits": null,
  "stop_bits": null,
  "tcp_port": null
 },
 "addressing": {
  "convention": "0-based",
  "note": "The manual lists generic Modbus data addresses in decimal and hex (e.g. Outlet Temp 2 (0x0002)) and states that BAS tools using 30001/40001 notation add 30001/40001 to them. The listed number is the wire address."
 },
 "default_word_order": "CDAB",
 "firmware": "C-More software 2.00 or higher",
 "source": {
  "title": "Modbus Communications User Manual - C-More Controller, Boiler Management System (BMS/BMS II), AERCO Control System (ACS)",
  "doc_number": "OMM-0035_D / GF-114",
  "revision_date": "2026-04-07",
  "url": "https://www.aerco.com/dfsmedia/0533dbba17714b1ab581ab07a4cbb521/58215-source/639112726810000000/omm-0035-0b-gf-114-modbus-communications-manual-05-18-12-0.pdf",
  "pages": "pp. 4-8 (requirements, function codes), 10-16 (C-More input and holding registers), 36-38 (password, direct drive, remote setpoint), 49-50 (termination/bias), 58-59 (setup), 70-75 (status messages, conversion equations)"
 },
 "notes": [
  "Temperature registers are raw counts (0-1000), not degrees, and are listed with scale 1 and units 'counts'. AERCO Table A-3 converts them with integer (floor) brackets: DEGREES_1 °F = floor((counts x 230 + 500) / 1000) + 20; DEGREES_2 °F = floor((counts x 330 + 500) / 1000) - 80; DEGREES_3 °F = floor((counts x 520 + 500) / 1000) + 40 (Celsius versions: x128 - 7, x183 - 62, x289 - 4). Because the result is rounded to whole degrees the conversion is a step function, not an exact scale/offset, so it is not encoded as one. Table A-4 gives the reverse (degrees to counts) for writes, e.g. DEGREES_1 counts = floor(((°F - 20) x 1000 + 115) / 230).",
  "ABS_DEG_1 registers (prop band, min/max load adjust, setpoint limit band) are temperature differences in counts (published range 0-1000): °F = floor((counts x 230 + 500) / 1000) for counts >= 0 and floor((counts x 230 - 500) / 1000) for counts < 0. They are typed INT16 because the manual gives a negative-count formula; within the published range INT16 and UINT16 read the same.",
  "Exhaust Temp (input 6) is printed as DEGREES_2 while Sensor Log Exhaust Temp (input 32, not listed) is DEGREES_3; check the conversion on site.",
  "32-bit counters (run cycles, run hours, fault-log cycle) are stored low word first (low word at the lower address), hence CDAB. The high word only ranges 0-15.",
  "The Unit Status / message code list is Table A-2 in the manual even though the register table points to Table A-1.",
  "Left out: reserved registers, the sensor-log and time-log history registers (28-43), the log pointers (68-70) and all BMS/BMS II/ACS registers (a separate controller covered in the same manual)."
 ],
